WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.677
Welcome to Module 13, Scalable S Q L with Spark and Microsoft Fabric.

00:00:06.677 --> 00:00:11.392
Before we move into Spark, think about what you already know.

00:00:11.392 --> 00:00:19.982
You have learned how to organize relational data, write S Q L queries, join tables, clean messy values, reshape data,

00:00:19.982 --> 00:00:26.912
use advanced analytical functions, and create reusable database views.

00:00:26.912 --> 00:00:30.754
Those ideas do not disappear when the data becomes larger.

00:00:31.402 --> 00:00:33.961
What changes is the computing environment.

00:00:33.961 --> 00:00:38.216
Spark can divide a large job across multiple workers.

00:00:38.216 --> 00:00:43.131
That gives us more computing power, but it also creates new questions.

00:00:43.131 --> 00:00:45.126
How is the data divided?

00:00:45.126 --> 00:00:47.866
When does data have to move between workers?

00:00:47.866 --> 00:00:50.866
Which operations create expensive shuffles?

00:00:50.866 --> 00:00:54.761
And when is Spark actually a better choice than a database?

00:00:54.761 --> 00:00:59.476
The goal of this module is not to turn you into a Spark administrator.

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:07.946
The goal is to help you carry your existing data-preparation skills into a distributed environment while keeping the

00:01:07.946 --> 00:01:13.061
same habits: know the grain, understand the schema, handle null values carefully, validate the output,

00:01:13.061 --> 00:01:16.665
and explain what the pipeline is doing.
